/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - By the method of creating supersaturation there are four classes: cooling crystallizers, for solutes whose solubility falls steeply with temperature; evaporative crystallizers, which boil off solvent with heat supplied by steam, for flat-so
   - The mixture of crystals and mother liquor in a crystallizer is the magma.
   - The yield follows from the solute and water balances with saturated mother liquor, as for one stage: crystals P = solute in feed − solute left in the mother liquor, with evaporated water and hydrate water taken out of the liquor first.
   - Crystals themselves are nearly pure, because impurities rarely fit the lattice.
   - Most salts release heat as they crystallize from solution (the heat of crystallization, roughly the negative of the heat of solution).
   - Q is the heat removed (kJ/h); F the feed (kg/h) of heat capacity cp, cooled from T1 to T2; P the crystals formed (kg/h); λc the heat of crystallization (kJ/kg, released); V the water evaporated and λv its latent heat at the crystallizer tem
   - In a cooling crystallizer the heat goes to cooling water through a jacket or coil, sized by Q = UAΔTlm.
   - A batch crystallizer is designed around its cooling (or evaporation) programme.
   - A continuous crystallizer is sized by residence time: volume = volumetric feed × τ, with τ chosen so the dominant product size reaches the target, as worked out in Crystal size distribution and the MSMPR crystallizer.
*/
