/* book Worked example: Q: 1000 kg/h of a solution with 30 mass% solute at 50 °C is cooled to 20 °C in a continuous cooling crystallizer. Solubility at 20 °C is 20 g per 100 g water; crystals are anhydrous. Take cp = 3.8 kJ/(kg K) and heat of crystallization 120 kJ/kg. Cooling water flows counter-current from 15 to 25 °C. Find the crystals, the heat removed, the cooling water and the area for U = 0.5 kW/(m² K).
   1. Feed: 300 kg/h solute, 700 kg/h water. Mother liquor holds 700 × 0.20 = 140 kg/h. Crystals P = 300 − 140 = 160 kg/h (53.3 % yield).
   2. Sensible heat = 1000 × 3.8 × (50 − 20) = 114 000 kJ/h. Heat of crystallization = 160 × 120 = 19 200 kJ/h.
   3. Q = 114 000 + 19 200 = 133 200 kJ/h = 133 200/3600 = 37.0 kW.
   4. Cooling water = 133 200/(4.18 × 10) = 3187 kg/h.
   5. End differences: 50 − 25 = 25 K at the hot end, 20 − 15 = 5 K at the cold end. ΔTlm = (25 − 5)/ln(25/5) = 20/1.609 = 12.43 K.
   6. A = Q/(UΔTlm) = 37.0/(0.5 × 12.43) = 5.95 m².
   Answer: 160 kg/h of crystals; 37.0 kW removed (14 % of it heat of crystallization); 3187 kg/h of cooling water; 5.95 m² of cooling surface. */
