/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A sieve analysis gives the mass of crystals between pairs of sieve openings.
   - The moments of n(L) give the totals: ∫n dL is the number of crystals, ∫nL² dL times ka their surface and ∫nL³ dL times ρkv their mass.
   - Assumptions: steady state; the magma is perfectly mixed, so every point has the same supersaturation and CSD; the product is withdrawn with the same CSD as the vessel; the feed holds no crystals; there is no breakage or agglomeration; and g
   - Take a magma volume V and the crystals between sizes L and L + ΔL.
   - n° is the population density of nuclei (L → 0), G the linear growth rate and τ the mean residence time.
   - NT is the number of crystals per unit volume, MT the magma density (mass of crystals per unit volume; use n° per metre with L in metres), kv the volume shape factor and ρ the crystal density.
   - With x = L/(Gτ), the mass fraction of product finer than L is W(x) = 1 − e−x(1 + x + x²/2 + x³/6).
   - Plotting ln B° against ln G from runs at several residence times gives the kinetic order i in B° = kNGi, with i = b/g.
   - To design, choose τ and read G and B° from the kinetics; the vessel volume is Qτ.
*/
