/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - J is usually in L/(m² h); Cp and Cf are the solute concentrations in permeate and feed; Qp and Qf the permeate and feed flows.
   - Solid membranes are dense or porous films of polymer (cellulose acetate, polyamide, polysulfone) or ceramic.
   - Membranes are packed into modules: plate-and-frame, tubular (for dirty feeds), spiral-wound (flat sheets with spacers rolled round a permeate tube, the usual RO module) and hollow fibre (thousands of fine tubes, the largest area per volume)
   - If a membrane permeable only to water separates pure water from a salt solution, water flows into the solution: osmosis.
   - π (Pa) for a dilute solution: i ions per formula unit (2 for NaCl), C in mol/m³, R = 8.314 J/(mol K), T in K.
   - As water leaves, the salt left behind piles up at the membrane surface: concentration polarisation.
   - Ultrafiltration membranes have real pores and separate by size, at 1–10 bar.
   - The held macromolecules collect at the surface until they form a gel layer of fixed concentration Cg.
   - An electrodialysis (ED) stack has many thin compartments between two electrodes, separated alternately by cation-exchange membranes (pass only cations) and anion-exchange membranes (pass only anions).
   - I is the current (A); z the charge of the ion (1 for NaCl); F = 96 485 C/mol; Q the diluate flow (m³/s); ΔC the drop in concentration (mol/m³); ε the current efficiency; N the number of cell pairs; V the stack voltage.
*/
