/* book Worked example: Q: An RO unit treats 1000 L/h of brackish water with 2000 mg/L NaCl at 25 °C and 75 % recovery; the permeate holds 100 mg/L. The membrane has Aw = 3.0 L/(m² h bar) and runs at ΔP = 15 bar. Find the permeate and retentate flows, the retentate concentration, the rejection, the osmotic pressures, the net driving pressure (using the mean feed-side osmotic pressure), the flux and the membrane area.
   1. Permeate Qp = 0.75 × 1000 = 750 L/h; retentate Qr = 250 L/h.
   2. Salt balance: 1000 × 2000 = 750 × 100 + 250 Cr, so Cr = (2 000 000 − 75 000)/250 = 7700 mg/L. Rejection R = 1 − 100/2000 = 95 %.
   3. π = 2CRT: feed C = 2000/58.44 = 34.2 mol/m³, π = 2 × 34.2 × 8.314 × 298 = 1.70 × 105 Pa = 1.70 bar. Retentate 7700 mg/L: 6.53 bar. Permeate 100 mg/L: 0.085 bar.
   4. Mean feed-side π = (1.70 + 6.53)/2 = 4.11 bar; Δπ = 4.11 − 0.085 = 4.03 bar. Net = 15 − 4.03 = 10.97 bar.
   5. Jw = 3.0 × 10.97 = 32.9 L/(m² h). Area = 750/32.9 = 22.8 m².
   Answer: 750 L/h permeate, 250 L/h retentate at 7700 mg/L; 95 % rejection; π = 1.70, 6.53 and 0.085 bar; net 10.97 bar; J = 32.9 L/(m² h); area 22.8 m². */
